Quake II PS3 update:
- v2.37 is a ready rollback for the v2.36 one-object raster-state experiment.
  It copies the exact v2.21/v2.35 EBOOT.BIN under higher 02.37 metadata so a
  physical console can recover immediately if v2.36 loses visible output.
  Install it only as a rollback; no code is compiled, relinked, or resigned and
  no configuration or game data is included. No RPCS3 test is used.
- v2.36 is the first performance experiment on the v2.21 hardware-video
  baseline. Of 138 retained objects, exactly one changes: `rsx_gl.o` is the
  isolated v2.24 backend object that filters redundant viewport and scissor
  methods only after converting requests to their actual physical target
  rectangles. It leaves v2.21's video selection, display buffers, GL1 camera
  and UI objects, stereo overlay, input, sound, networking, OSK, gameplay, and
  font path byte-identical. USB input, the post-v2.21 stereo-blend override, and
  the scene-scale bridge are intentionally not linked. This is a serial-built,
  update-only physical test and is not tested with RPCS3.
- v2.35 establishes the new physical-hardware compatibility baseline after the
  owner confirmed that v2.21 still renders and plays correctly while v2.32,
  forced-720p v2.33, and the byte-identical-v2.27 v2.34 recovery remain black
  with live audio/input. It copies v2.21's accepted EBOOT.BIN byte-for-byte and
  raises only PARAM.SFO to 02.35. Nothing is rebuilt, relinked, or resigned, and
  no autoexec, configuration, game data, save, or other installed file is
  included. All future performance experiments branch from this executable and
  change one independently auditable object at a time. No RPCS3 test is used.
- v2.34 is a metadata-only executable bisect after v2.33 remained black while
  audio and blind menu input continued under forced 720p with archived stereo
  disabled. It copies the complete, previously accepted v2.27 EBOOT.BIN
  byte-for-byte and raises only PARAM.SFO to 02.34 so it can install over newer
  recovery attempts. Nothing is compiled, relinked, signed again, or mixed from
  another renderer line. This removes v2.33's safe-video object as well as the
  v2.28/v2.32 tiled scene-resolve delta. The package contains no autoexec,
  configuration, game data, saves, or other installed files and is not tested
  with RPCS3.
- v2.33 is a visible-output recovery update for the physical-console case where
  v2.32 remains alive (audio and blind menu input work) but produces a black
  screen. It restores the complete physically accepted v2.27 linked-object
  baseline, then changes only its PS3 video object to reuse the previously
  successful v2.03 recovery selector: request standard 1280x720 output, disable
  archived stereoscopic output for that launch, and fall back to the current XMB
  mode only when 720p is unavailable. The v2.04 RGBW diagnostic framebuffer is
  deliberately not enabled because that separate experiment exited to XMB on
  hardware. No autoexec, config, game data, saves, or other installed files are
  included. This is an update-only physical recovery package, serial-built with
  the original GCC 7.2.0/PSL1GHT toolchain and not tested with RPCS3.
- v2.32 resumes from the original computer's exact v2.28 two-object tiled
  scene-resolve line after the returned-machine v2.29-v2.31 reconstructions
  failed physical startup or load. The private scene remains a full-pitch
  linear allocation and the destination remains the established tiled display
  eye; compact allocations, private tiling/Z-cull, target-aware policy changes,
  startup commands, and configuration payloads are not imported. The one code
  change conservatively retires the private color-target producer before the
  tiled render-target-to-texture resolve. This establishes a hardware-safe
  first test of v2.28's strongest fill-rate lever before attempting to remove
  synchronization. Linear output and every unrelated v2.28 object are
  byte-identical. The update contains only PARAM.SFO and USRDIR/EBOOT.BIN, was
  built with the original GCC 7.2.0/PSL1GHT toolchain, and is not tested with
  RPCS3.
- v2.28 extends the adaptive scene-resolution bridge to effective tiled and
  tiled-plus-Z-cull display targets. The reduced scene remains in a private
  linear RSX color/depth allocation, then one ordinary fixed-shader quad
  samples it into the restored tiled eye before native-resolution HUD/UI work.
  This avoids the tile-incompatible 2D transfer destination which previously
  forced those modes back to full-resolution scene shading. Linear output
  retains v2.27's accepted scale-engine path. `ps3_rsx_scene_tiled_resolve 0`
  is a session-only recovery switch, and `ps3_rsx_scene_sync 2` adds a
  conservative pre-resolve idle diagnostic. The Makefile now applies the
  bridge's two required object-symbol rewrites as part of their compile rules,
  preventing a source rebuild from silently disabling display-surface
  tracking. Only the native RSX backend and scene bridge changed; GL1, stereo
  blend, UI, online/OSK, input, sound, gameplay, and the direct native path are
  preserved. This is an update-only package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.27 removes the redundant pre-blit RSX idle drain from the adaptive scene
  resolve. The conservative post-blit 2D-to-3D barrier remains enabled, and
  `ps3_rsx_scene_sync 2` restores both barriers for recovery testing. Mode 0
  is an explicit FIFO-only diagnostic. The accepted direct renderer is
  unaffected when scene scaling is disabled.
- v2.26 adds bounded physical-cadence feedback to v2.25's scene resolver. With
  `ps3_rsx_scene_scale 0` and `ps3_rsx_scene_adaptive 1`, Custom starts at
  native scene resolution, 720p Performance starts at 75%, and 1080p Quality
  starts at two-thirds. Two consecutive gameplay cadences above 40 ms prove
  that presentation has fallen from the 33.3 ms target to the 50 ms VBlank
  tier; the scene then steps down through 87.5%, 75%, 66.7%, 58.3%, and 50%
  bounds as applicable. It never oscillates quality upward during a gameplay
  session. Explicit scene scales remain deterministic and disable adaptation,
  while `ps3_rsx_scene_adaptive 0` retains the fixed profile defaults. UI and
  scanout resolution, stereo routing, allocation/tiled/z-trick fallbacks, and
  every accepted v2.25 object are unchanged. This is an update-only package
  and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.25 adds a scene-resolution path with enough fill-rate leverage to move a
  20 FPS 720p workload back toward the 33.3 ms presentation budget. The 720p
  Performance profile now renders only the 3D refdef at 75% linear dimensions
  (normally 960x540) in a private RSX color/depth surface, resolves it with the
  hardware scale engine into the unchanged 1280x720 eye, and then draws HUD,
  menus, console, text, OSK and display filters at native output resolution.
  The 1080p Quality profile uses a two-thirds scene scale (approximately
  1280x720) while Custom remains native by default. `ps3_rsx_scene_scale 0`
  selects this profile-aware policy; explicit values from 0.50 through 1.00
  override it live, and 1.00 disables the path. The bridge wraps the accepted
  v2.24 exports instead of recompiling its GL1 frontend. Allocation failure,
  tiled targets, and legacy z-trick automatically retain the proven direct
  renderer. Stereo eyes have independent clears and resolves, while the v2.23
  blend correction, USB input, online/OSK behavior, sound and gameplay remain
  unchanged. This is an update-only package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.24 keeps the accepted v2.23 executable and its corrected frame-packed
  color blend, then restores only the independently cleared v1.99 native RSX
  raster-state optimization. Viewport and scissor requests are compared after
  conversion to their actual physical target rectangles, so repeated mono and
  same-eye methods are omitted while every real frame-packed/top-bottom eye
  transition is still emitted. The earlier physical one-object bisect located
  v1.99's rendering regression in the separate GL1 camera-matrix object; that
  object remains on the proven v1.98 baseline here. UI transforms and text,
  menus, lighting, filters, input, networking, sound and gameplay are otherwise
  byte-for-byte v2.23. This is an update-only package and is not tested in
  RPCS3.
- v2.23 corrects the v2.22 stereo-blend integration after physical frame-packed
  output proved the first eye was still running the legacy opaque overlay. The
  preserved v2.20 GL1 object had inlined R_Flash into RI_RenderFrame and called
  a private R_PolyBlend helper directly, bypassing v2.22's public replacement.
  The single PPC64 REL24 call site now resolves to the stereo-safe active-eye
  2D overlay, whose standard alpha blend is therefore actually executed for
  underwater, damage and power-up tints. USB keyboard/mouse support and every
  other v2.22 object are unchanged. This is an update-only package and is not
  tested in RPCS3.
- v2.22 keeps the accepted v2.20 executable baseline and adds two isolated PS3
  compatibility bridges. Native USB keyboards now provide bindable raw-key
  events plus layout-aware console, menu, chat and multiplayer text entry;
  native USB mice provide five buttons, wheel input and Quake mouse-look using
  the existing sensitivity, filtering, freelook and strafe settings. Both are
  optional, hot-pluggable and suppressed while the system OSK owns input. The
  underwater, damage and power-up color blend is now a deterministic 2D quad
  in the active stereo eye with an explicit alpha blend function, replacing
  the inherited perspective-space overlay that could saturate the left eye.
  Pad, Move, online/OSK, sound, gameplay and renderer objects otherwise remain
  on the proven baseline. v2.21's ineffective per-draw font sampler diagnostic
  is intentionally omitted while v2.20's low-cost atlas setup is retained.
  This is an update-only package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.21 is a ready fallback for the v2.20 scaled-font test and is intended only
  if v2.20 still shows corrupted 2x/3x text. It retains v2.20's two-object
  nearest-atlas fix and additionally reasserts nearest sampling after binding
  `conchars` for each native string or individual glyph draw. The native RSX
  state filter discards already-current sampler writes, limiting the normal
  cost to the frontend checks. No renderer geometry, transforms, video, input,
  sound, networking, gameplay, or OSK behavior changes; all non-font objects
  remain byte-for-byte v2.19. This is an update-only package and is not tested
  in RPCS3.
- v2.20 keeps the physically proven v2.19 executable baseline and changes only
  the two GL1 font-sampler objects. Physical v2.19 capture confirms that the
  console and menu font is clean at 1x UI scale but becomes internally doubled
  and smeared as UI scale increases. The tightly packed `pics/conchars.pcx`
  atlas now uses nearest filtering when it is loaded and is kept on nearest
  filtering after later texture-mode changes, preventing adjacent glyph cells
  from bleeding into scaled characters. Renderer geometry, video transforms,
  input, sound, networking, gameplay, the working multiplayer OSK persistence,
  and all other objects remain byte-for-byte v2.19. This is an update-only
  package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.19 abandons the post-v1.98 renderer experiment and restores the exact
  physically proven v1.98 object baseline. The RSX renderer, GL1 frontend,
  video handoff, input, sound, networking, gameplay, and every other object are
  byte-for-byte v1.98. The sole replacement is the menu object containing the
  multiplayer address-book completion: an accepted native PS3 OSK result is
  copied into its `adr#` cvar, marked archived, and written to configuration
  immediately; leaving the address book with the controller also commits all
  fields before Refresh reads and pings them. The existing v1.98 asynchronous
  system OSK and URL keyboard remain otherwise unchanged. This is an
  update-only package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.18 removes the native UI's dependence on retained projection constants.
  Physical v2.17 testing isolated two different transforms: the initial menu
  was upright but still clipped by the old small canvas, while the same menu
  became correctly positioned but vertically inverted after a world frame.
  Native console, menu, HUD, text, and filter vertices now convert Quake's
  top-left y-down coordinates directly to RSX clip space and use an identity
  shader matrix. This makes the 2D transform deterministic at initial boot and
  after every 3D-to-2D transition without changing the world projection or the
  generic compatibility glOrtho path. The package is update-only and is not
  tested in RPCS3.
- v2.17 corrects the native 2D projection orientation exposed by v2.16's real
  1920x1080 canvas. Physical output proved the console prompt submitted at
  y=1045 appeared at the top of a 1080-line frame, while the main-menu artwork
  was mirrored and displaced toward the bottom. The RSX viewport already
  performs OpenGL's Y conversion, so the desktop-style y-down orthographic
  matrix was applying a second inversion. Native console, menu, HUD, and filter
  quads now use the complementary 2D projection; the 3D world projection and
  the v2.16 native-resolution handoff are unchanged. The package is update-only
  and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.16 fixes the native renderer's PS3 video handoff after physical v2.15
  testing proved both interleaved and planar immutable text streams produce the
  same image with correct caller geometry. The trace exposed the actual fault:
  a 1920x1080 RSX target was still using Yamagi's default 640x480 logical
  canvas, so every 8x8 glyph was non-uniformly stretched to roughly 24x18
  physical pixels and dense rows looked enlarged and overprinted. Native RSX
  now gives the engine, UI projection, viewport, and scanout target identical
  dimensions; stereoscopic output retains its required 1280x720 per-eye
  canvas. This removes the ineffective low-resolution coordinate stretch--it
  never reduced RSX fill cost--while preserving the update's existing native
  renderer and planar glyph path. The package is update-only and is not tested
  in RPCS3.
- v2.15 moves native console, menu, HUD, and inventory glyphs from the generic
  40-byte interleaved vertex format to separate tightly packed position, UV,
  and color arrays in the existing flip-fenced GPU-local ring. This matches
  PSL1GHT's physical debug-font renderer and directly removes the last
  text-specific vertex-fetch variable after the caller-side row batching was
  verified against upstream and old affected builds proved the corruption
  predates that batching API. A one-shot hardware trace records the actual
  glyph scale, virtual dimensions, viewport, and orthographic coefficients so
  the next physical result can distinguish bad input geometry from RSX fetch
  corruption. The package is update-only, contains three entries, and is not
  tested in RPCS3.
- v2.14 keeps v2.13's flip-fenced addressable glyph stream and adds the missing
  installed-configuration migration revealed by its first physical result.
  The long vertical immediate-command trails are gone, but v1.98's archived
  1080p automatic `r_consolescale` now applies cleanly and makes the 8x8 font
  enormous. PS3 console-scale revision 3 resets every existing update install
  once to 1x, after which explicit user changes remain authoritative. The
  focused build retains v2.13 byte-for-byte except for cl_screen.o, uses the
  required strip -> `sprxlinker` -> NPDRM path, contains three update entries,
  and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.13 replaces the remaining high-volume command-inline glyph path with one
  complete addressable triangle list per console, menu, HUD, or inventory
  string. Physical v2.12 testing showed the same text corruption with the exact
  v1.98 EBOOT, proving that v1.98 was a stable gameplay baseline rather than a
  clean text baseline and invalidating the later camera-object attribution.
  The vertical glyph trails match the one subsystem still capable of emitting
  tens of thousands of immediate RSX method words during a full console redraw.
  Text now uses the existing flip-fenced rotating GPU-local UI ring: every
  string receives immutable, fresh-address vertices until the display fence,
  and the command buffer carries one normal array draw rather than 42 method
  words per glyph. The focused physical package retains every v1.98 object
  byte-for-byte except rsx_gl.o, and keeps the established compatibility camera
  matrix. It is update-only, contains three entries, and is not tested in
  RPCS3.
- v2.12 removes v1.99's direct camera-basis world-view matrix optimization
  after the corrected v2.11 one-object physical bisect reproduced the enlarged,
  overlapping and vertically streaked text with that object alone. The
  renderer again constructs the view through the established GL matrix
  operations and reads back the resulting compatibility matrix, exactly as in
  the physically clean v1.98 build. The v1.99 resolved physical
  viewport/scissor cache is exonerated by this bisect and remains eligible for
  later performance builds. This immediate recovery package carries the exact
  physically clean v1.98 EBOOT under 02.12 metadata so it cannot introduce an
  untested source or link delta. It is update-only, contains three entries, and
  is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.11 corrects the v2.10 package construction error without changing its
  executable object set. v2.10 passed the raw top-level linker ELF directly to
  `make_self_npdrm`, skipping PSL1GHT's required strip and `sprxlinker` pass;
  that leaves invalid import-descriptor function counts and makes GameOS return
  to XMB before `main`. This package signs the already stripped and sprxlinked
  `build-rsx-v210/q2ps3.elf` instead. It remains the same focused visual bisect:
  the physically clean v1.98 object baseline with only v1.99's direct
  camera/world-view matrix `gl1_main.o`, while `rsx_gl.o` remains exactly v1.98.
  It is an update-only three-entry physical test package and is not tested in
  RPCS3.
- v2.10 is the first one-object v1.99 visual-regression bisect. Its complete
  object set comes from the physically clean v1.98 build except for v1.99's
  gl1_main.o, which contains the direct camera/world-view matrix optimization;
  rsx_gl.o remains exactly v1.98, so resolved physical viewport/scissor
  filtering is absent. The build uses the restored legacy gem+SPURS/no-camera
  library combination. Before replacing gl1_main.o, relinking the untouched
  v1.98 objects produced a q2ps3.elf.map byte-for-byte identical to the original
  v1.98 map, validating object order, addresses, archive selection, and imports.
  Malformed text in v2.10 implicates the camera-matrix change; clean text
  implicates v1.99's rsx_gl raster filtering instead. This is an update-only
  three-entry physical bisect package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.09 is the exact v1.98 executable under higher update metadata. Physical
  testing showed the same installation and configuration render normally on
  v1.97 and v1.98, while the byte-identical v1.99 EBOOT repackaged as v2.08
  boots but immediately produces enlarged, overlapping console glyphs. That
  disproves the scale-only diagnosis and isolates the visual regression to the
  two v1.99 object changes: direct camera-matrix construction in gl1_main and
  resolved physical viewport/scissor filtering in rsx_gl. v2.09 compiles and
  relinks nothing; its EBOOT.BIN is copied byte-for-byte from the physically
  clean v1.98 update so the title can return to the last good visual baseline.
  It is an update-only three-entry package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.08 is a byte-for-byte executable recovery package after both v2.06 and
  v2.07 returned to XMB before `main`, regardless of whether the SELF included
  or omitted the Move/Eye libraries. It reuses the exact EBOOT.BIN from the
  physically boot-proven v2.05/v1.99 package without compiling or relinking a
  single object; only the update metadata is raised to 02.08. This deliberately
  withdraws the v2.06 console migration and all v2.00+ restoration changes so
  the installed title can return to a known-working executable. It remains an
  update-only three-entry package and is not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.07 is the higher-version startup recovery after v2.06 returned directly
  to XMB without creating a new boot trace, proving the process never reached
  `main` and therefore never entered the restored v1.99 renderer. It keeps the
  byte-identical v1.99 RSX object, v2.06's one-time 1x console migration, OSK,
  and persistent address-book handling, but selects the link-time Move stub and
  omits libgem, libcamera, and SPURS from the SELF. This isolates the pre-main
  import failure while preserving the visible-renderer and configuration fixes.
  PS Move tracking is intentionally unavailable in this recovery package. It is
  update-only, serial-built, and not tested in RPCS3.
- v2.06 returns to the physically boot-proven v1.99 RSX presentation object
  while restoring v2.01's boot-quarantined PS Move/Eye implementation and
  persistent multiplayer address-book handling around it. It deliberately
  excludes v2.00's per-string Begin/End renderer change, which the v2.05 cold
  bisect isolated as the black-output regression. A second one-time PS3 console
  scale migration resets carried automatic or oversized values to 1x: at
  1920x1080 Yamagi's automatic value is 3x, exactly explaining the full-width
  80-character lines and enormous glyphs seen in the v2.05 hardware capture.
  Future explicit scale changes remain persistent. This is an update-only
  physical-console build; no RPCS3 run is used.
- v2.05 is a physical cold-boot bisect package containing the exact untouched
  v1.99 executable under higher install metadata. v1.50 boots after a console
  reboot while v2.01 still produces black with only a DualShock 3 after the
  same reboot, so this cleanly tests the boundary immediately before v2.00's
  camera/Move, text-isolation and address-book changes. No objects are rebuilt
  or mixed for this package. It is update-only and no RPCS3 run is used.
- v2.04 adds a pre-renderer scanout diagnostic to the v2.03 recovery image.
  Immediately after forced-720p video configuration it allocates and registers
  a minimal linear framebuffer, fills it directly from the PPU with horizontal
  red/green/blue/white bars, queues buffer zero through GCM, verifies flip
  completion with a bounded wait, and holds the pattern for two seconds before
  Quake initializes. This bypasses game shaders, textures, matrices, menus,
  filters, batching and the normal frame scheduler, distinguishing a video/GCM
  scanout failure from black content generated by the renderer. Move remains
  link-time isolated. This is an update-only physical diagnostic build and no
  RPCS3 run is used.
- v2.03 is the true renderer recovery build. The v2.02 physical trace proves
  that startup, 1080p video, RSX initialization, the main loop, menu submission
  and the first queued frame all succeed, narrowing the remaining black output
  to presentation changes introduced after the last physically proven v1.96.
  This build pins the complete renderer/video/pacing object set to the exact
  v1.96 binaries except for a recovery-only mode selector that forces standard
  720p, disables archived stereo for that launch, and prevents the title/XMB
  1080p state seen in the failing trace from being reused. It retains the
  current boot-quarantined controller input and
  persistent address-book menu objects, and keeps the link-time Move stub so
  archived settings or attached hardware cannot enter GEM. The v1.97 exact-2D
  projection cache and v1.99 raster/matrix cache are therefore absent. This is
  an update-only physical recovery build and no RPCS3 run is used.
- v2.02 is a boot-recovery and import-isolation build after physical hardware
  showed that v2.00/v2.01 failed before `main` regardless of attached devices.
  It retains the renderer/text and address-book changes, but selects a link-time
  Move stub and omits libgem, libcamera and SPURS from the SELF entirely. This
  provides a higher-version recovery update for installations already on v2.01
  and tests the only new loader-level structural delta from the last bootable
  images: the camera SPRX import. Move is deliberately unavailable in this one
  recovery build. No RPCS3 run is used and the package remains update-only.
- v2.01 quarantines libgem and libcamera from the boot path. An archived
  `psmove_enable 1` can no longer initialize either module before the first
  visible frame merely because a Move wand, Navigation controller, or Eye is
  already connected; Move starts only from its in-menu enable/calibrate/reinit
  actions or the matching console commands. This makes a direct v1.50-to-v2.01
  update safe with devices attached. The GEM SPURS configuration is also
  corrected from v2.00's single worker/single runnable workload to the five
  workers and five priorities required by the working PSL1GHT samples. Those
  resources exist only while Move is explicitly active and are released on
  disable or shutdown. Physical-console validation remains required and no
  RPCS3 run is used. This is an update-only release.
- v2.00 replaces the PS3 port's inertial-only PS Move placeholder with a real
  sphere-controller backend. It distinguishes the Move wand from the Navigation
  controller through `gemGetInfo`, opens a PlayStation Eye in 640x480 RAW8 at
  60 Hz, submits timestamped camera frames through `gemUpdateStart/Finish`,
  selects a trackable hue, and uses the camera-corrected angular velocity with
  raw gyro fallback when the sphere is temporarily hidden. The gamepad / Move
  menu adds `calibrate / recenter PS Move`; its native alignment overlay reports
  missing wand/Eye, color selection, calibration, lost tracking, and ready
  states, and pressing MOVE starts calibration while the prompt is active.
  `psmove_status`, `psmove_calibrate`, and `psmove_reinit` provide explicit
  recovery and logging. Camera/container/module ownership is released on every
  failure and normal shutdown, and GEM retains one application SPU.
  Console/menu text now treats each string as an independent RSX Begin/End
  primitive instead of retaining v1.96's stream across rows, and the tightly
  packed `conchars` atlas is forced to nearest filtering both at load and after
  later texture-mode changes. This intentionally gives correctness priority
  over the small text-only draw-call saving. Address-book OSK acceptance now
  commits the edited `adr#` cvar and configuration immediately; Circle/Select
  also commits every field by checking the translated PS3 menu key, fixing the
  old controller exit path that silently discarded entries before Refresh
  could ping them. The native target serial-builds and links libcamera; physical
  Move/Eye and text validation remain required. This is an update-only release;
  no RPCS3 testing is used.
- v1.99 removes the native camera-matrix rebuild from every rendered eye.
  `R_SetupFrame` already computes the forward/right/up basis for frustum and
  view work; the RSX path now writes the mathematically identical column-major
  world-view matrix directly from that basis, eliminating a second set of five
  trigonometric rotations, six generic 4x4 multiplies, and the compatibility
  matrix readback. A 500,000-camera algebraic comparison stayed below
  7.3e-12 absolute error, and a separate 250,000-case PS3-style Float32 sweep
  over +/-8192-unit origins stayed below 7.8e-7 orientation and 0.0081-unit
  translation error. The backend also caches resolved physical viewport and
  scissor rectangles, so identical RSX raster methods are omitted while real
  mono/stereo eye or depth-range changes still emit; a 500,000-transition
  state simulation produced zero final-state mismatches. Statistics expose
  these saves as `raster_hits`. The universal 30 FPS floor is now measured at
  the real rounded 33,334 us budget rather than the former loose 34 ms proxy,
  explicit/automatic 60 FPS is measured at 16,667 us rather than 20 ms, and
  `floor_streak_max` reports consecutive floor misses. Old 20/34 ms fields
  remain for comparison with archived logs. All changes operate beneath
  Custom/720p/1080p, mono/stereo, filters, framebuffer layouts, quality
  settings, and cap selection; no displayed settings profile is detected or
  favored. v1.98's same-eye `ui_dupes` evidence remains active for the pending
  physical stale-text diagnosis. This is an update-only physical-test release;
  no RPCS3 testing is used.
- v1.98 makes the remaining compatibility-lightmap pass sparse. World and
  brush surfaces mark only the lightmap pages they actually occupy; static
  blending and show-triangle diagnostics visit those pages in the same
  ascending order, while their persistent heads are cleared sparsely at the
  start of the next pass. A fully single-pass scene now skips the empty legacy
  pass before changing depth, blend, texture, or dynamic-atlas state. This
  removes the former 128-head clear, 127-page scan, and empty dynamic setup
  from common frames without changing dynamic lightmaps, debug modes, page
  ordering, or recovery after an interrupted pass. A 200,000-pass randomized
  test covering 4,804,914 surface insertions found zero chain, ordering, or
  stale-head mismatches. The sole native glyph path also gains stats-only,
  per-eye exact-call signatures: `ps3_rsx_stats 1` reports `ui_dupes`, allowing
  a physical log to distinguish duplicate console/menu submission from stale
  presentation without suppressing legitimate draws or adding normal-play
  hashing cost. A 10,000-eye simulation detected all 320,000 injected exact
  duplicates with no changed-position false positives or hash collisions.
  Both changes are independent of Custom/720p/1080p, mono/stereo, filters,
  framebuffer layout, quality, and frame cap. This is an update-only
  physical-test release; no RPCS3 testing is used.
- v1.97 replaces the repeated native LoadIdentity/Ortho/LoadIdentity sequence
  at frame start, post-world 2D setup, and display-filter resolve with one
  exact 2D projection setup. The new path builds the same standard Quake II
  matrix, selects the same UI stream, leaves GL_MODELVIEW selected, and copies
  the final projection and identity modelview directly while advancing matrix
  state once instead of as many as three times. When those exact matrices are
  already resident and state filtering is enabled, the matrix operation is a
  complete no-op; `ps3_rsx_stats 1` exposes those saves as `ortho_hits`.
