  720p/60 and 1080p/30 acceptance gate.
- v1.69 extends paired-eye MD2 reuse to projected-shadow expansion. For an
  ordinary lit entity with v1.67's retained BSP light sample, the first eye now
  stores the final untextured shadow primitive stream in the same fixed one-MiB
  arena used by interpolation and model commands. The second eye retains its
  own culling, shadow matrix, blend/depth state, and RSX draw submission but no
  longer walks the MD2 command list or repeats the per-vertex projection math.
  Fullbright, shell, no-lightdata, second-eye-only, and arena-overflow cases
  retain the original path. Main MD2 and shadow client-array state is also
  enabled once per entity instead of once per primitive. The new shadow fields
  add exactly three KiB of BSS and no second arena. The native OSK, direct
  rotating UI stream, and opt-in telemetry remain active. This is an
  update-only physical-test release.
- v1.68 removes the remaining eye-independent MD2 command expansion from the
  paired native stereo pass. The first eye now retains the final primitive
  type, position, texture-coordinate, and lit-color streams in v1.67's same
  fixed one-MiB arena; the second eye submits those streams through its own
  matrix, depth, and RSX draw path without walking the model command list or
  rebuilding vertex colors. Interpolation and expanded commands share that
  arena, so this adds only about three KiB of descriptor state rather than a
  second large buffer. Per-entity overflow rolls back partial command data and
  immediately uses the original scratch path. Mono output, eye-specific
  culling, projected shadows, and all draw ordering remain unchanged. The
  native OSK and opt-in stage telemetry remain active. This is an update-only
  physical-test release.
- v1.67 removes eye-independent MD2 work from the paired native stereo pass.
  The first eye now retains normal entity light samples, shade orientation,
  shadow light spots, and model-space interpolated vertices; matching entities
  visible to the second eye reuse those results while still performing their
  own eye-specific culling, matrices, depth, projected-shadow submission, and
  RSX draws. A fixed one-MiB vertex arena is consumed by actual visible model
  sizes, with roughly 11 KiB of descriptors; overflow and entities visible in
  only one eye use the original path. Maps without BSP light data are excluded
  from lighting reuse because the legacy fallback intentionally leaves the
  shadow sample position unchanged. Mono output is untouched. v1.66's opaque
  animation cache, the native OSK, and opt-in stage telemetry remain active.
  This is an update-only physical-test release.
- v1.66 caches animated-texture resolution for native opaque world surfaces.
  Quake II resolves an animation by walking its linked texinfo chain for every
  surface; the native path now retains that result for the current animation
  frame and registration generation, sharing it across repeated surfaces and
  the paired stereo eye. Loader-time validation permanently excludes any chain
  containing a translucent frame, preserving the original alpha path's
  order-sensitive texinfo mutation, and static textures retain their direct
  path. The cache exists only in native-RSX model data and is bounded by the
  map format to 128 KiB. v1.65 culling, v1.64 telemetry, v1.63 stereo-light
  reuse, and v1.62's asynchronous native OSK remain active. This is an
  update-only physical-test release; `ps3_rsx_stats 1` remains the performance
  acceptance gate.
- v1.65 specializes native frustum rejection for the answer the renderer
  actually consumes. The general box/plane routine projected both extreme
  corners to classify front, back, or spanning; GL1 only rejects a node when
  its most-forward support corner is still behind a frustum plane. The native
  path now selects and projects that one corner directly, halving the
  multiply/add work and avoiding the general out-of-line classifier for every
  BSP node and culled entity. All comparisons and equality boundaries are
  identical; an exhaustive one-million-case sign-mask test produced zero
  classification differences. v1.64 stage telemetry, v1.63 stereo light reuse,
  the native OSK, and the direct rotating UI stream remain active. This is an
  update-only package; physical telemetry remains the final performance gate.
- v1.64 extends the opt-in physical-console telemetry with PPU stage
  attribution. Every 120 completed frames, `ps3_rsx_stats 1` now emits a
  second `PPU stages` record containing average and worst-case microseconds for
  dynamic-light marking, view/PVS setup, world submission, entities,
  particles/dynamic-light effects, alpha/flash work, the complete measured
  scene, and remaining renderer/UI work. Stereo eye passes accumulate into one
  displayed-frame sample, so the numbers align with the existing cadence and
  720p/60 or 1080p/30 target-miss record. Normal play performs no timestamp
  calls while stats are disabled. v1.63's stereo light reuse, v1.62's OSK, and
  v1.60's direct rotating local UI stream are unchanged. This is an update-only
  diagnostic release intended to identify the next measured optimization.
- v1.63 replaces v1.62's never-eligible stereo BSP replay experiment with a
  smaller correctness-safe optimization. Native frame-packed rendering now
  identifies the paired second eye at the renderer frame boundary and reuses
  the first eye's dynamic-light BSP and inline brush-model marks. Each eye
  still advances its own surface-visibility generation and performs its own
  BSP/frustum walk, preserving its offset camera origin, PVS, backface choice,
  translucent order, and geometry. Dynamic-light generation is now explicitly
  separate from the visibility generation on all render paths; mono output is
  unchanged. The inert 256-KiB replay cache and its inaccurate trace claim are
  removed. v1.62's native OSK and v1.60's direct rotating local text stream are
  unchanged. This is an update-only package; physical target-miss telemetry
  remains the 720p/60 and 1080p/30 acceptance gate.
- v1.62 adds the native PS3 on-screen keyboard to every editable menu
  field. Cross/Enter opens an asynchronous system dialog with the existing
  value prefilled: server-address entries use the URL layout, server limits
  use the numeric keypad, and player/host names use the full keyboard. The
  dialog uses a temporary four-MiB container, returns it only after the
  system's unloaded event, consumes pad input while open, and never blocks
  the engine/sysutil loop. The v1.60 direct local text path remains unchanged.
  This is an update-only package; physical `target_misses` telemetry remains
  the 30/60 FPS gate.
- v1.61 reduces native entity submission work without changing visible model
  order or geometry. A single stable encounter-order list records translucent
  entities while solid entities are visited, eliminating the second scan of
  the frame entity array and avoiding depth-write state changes when the list
  is empty. Fully off-frustum sprites and beams are rejected before texture,
  blend, matrix, and vertex work, while visible sprites use the renderer's
  direct textured-fan path rather than the generic client-array decoder.
  v1.60's direct rotating GPU-local console/menu stream remains byte-for-byte
  unchanged. The 720p/60 and 1080p/30 targets still require physical-console
  telemetry confirmation. This is an update-only package for an existing
  installation.
- v1.60 removes the asynchronous main-to-local copy from the preferred physical
  console/menu/HUD vertex path. Completed 2D batches are now packed directly at
  their final address in the existing 16 MiB rotating GPU-local ring, followed
  by the established per-batch vertex-cache invalidate. The completed flip
  still fences every address before ring reuse. This prevents a vertex fetch
  from racing the asynchronous transfer that populated its local destination,
  eliminates one complete copy and its XDR cache publication, and adds no RSX
  memory beyond v1.59. `ps3_rsx_ui_local_stage 1` retains the DMA-staged path
  for A/B testing, while zero retains direct XDR; allocation pressure falls
  back automatically. v1.59's sparse world traversal and target-miss telemetry
  remain intact. This is an update-only package for an existing installation.
- v1.59 removes two fixed-range scans from each native world pass. BSP
  traversal now records the sparse set of base textures that actually received
  visible surfaces, then consumes that set in the same ascending material order
  instead of checking all 1,024 registered-image slots. Combined-lightmap
  buckets likewise visit only occupied pages while retaining ascending page
  order. Geometry, surface linkage, lighting, shaders, and fallback behavior
  are unchanged. Opt-in `ps3_rsx_stats 1` reports the active 720p/60 or
  1080p/30 target, cadence samples, and target misses every 120 frames for
  direct physical-console verification. v1.58's rotating GPU-local text ring
  and offscreen UI rejection remain unchanged. This is an update-only package
  for an existing installation.
- v1.58 fixes a same-address reuse gap in the GPU-local console/menu staging
  path. The PPU-side XDR construction stream already rotated between frames,
  but the immutable local destination still restarted at offset zero every
  vblank, recreating the physical RSX vertex-fetch cache pattern that rotation
  was intended to avoid. The preferred local stage is now a 16 MiB flip-fenced
  ring that leaves one complete 8 MiB worst-case frame budget before wrapping;
  allocation pressure automatically falls back to the previous 8 MiB staged
  path, then to direct XDR only if both local allocations fail. Fully offscreen
  2D quads, strings, and individual glyphs are rejected before vertex packing,
  cache publication, transfer, and fetch. Scaled consoles also stop walking
  rows that cannot reach the viewport. These changes reduce PPU work and UI
  transfer bandwidth while preserving glyph geometry, texture coordinates,
  draw order, shaders, and the 720p/60 and 1080p/30 performance targets. This
  is an update-only package for an existing installation.
- v1.57 adds optional native RSX presentation filters after the complete scene,
  HUD, console, and menu. Scanlines, RGB aperture grille, vignette, CRT soft,
  and CRT strong are generated procedurally in one multiplicative fragment pass
  per eye; strength is live from 0.0 to 2.0, and Off performs no draw or shader
  work. Stereo applies the pass before closing each eye, preserving both HDMI
  frame packing and top/bottom routing. Video Options also exposes texture
  filtering, dynamic lighting, screen color blends, and 0/2x/4x overbright;
  the native renderer's nonfunctional one-sample MSAA selector is hidden.
  Performance presets now select real scanout targets and all proven batching,
  state-cache, texture-lifetime, combined-lightmap, and staged-UI switches: 720p
  is paired with a 60 FPS cap, while native 1080p is paired with a paced 30 FPS
  cap and safely falls back to the XMB mode if unavailable. Tiled targets and
  Z-cull remain conservative manual diagnostics. v1.54's GPU-local staged text
  transfer, complete-range cache publication, immutable draw source, and
  flip-fenced destination reuse remain intact pending physical validation. The
  release is an update-only package for an existing v1.50 installation.
- v1.56 reduces PPU submission work in the native combined-lighting path while
  leaving v1.54's GPU-local staged console/menu stream, vertex layout, shaders,
  and visual decisions unchanged. Each non-empty base-texture/lightmap-page
  bucket now prepares the combined program, sampler pair, stream mode, indexed
  mode, and overbright scale once; its BSP fans then use a prepared append path
  instead of repeating those invariant checks per surface. Empty page slots are
  skipped, inline brush models retain the standalone path, and material changes
  remain batch boundaries. The release is a two-file update package for an
  existing v1.50 installation; physical-console text and pacing validation are
  still required.
- v1.55 keeps v1.54's GPU-local staged UI path byte-for-byte intact and removes
  duplicate PPU work left in v1.53's combined world-chain fallback. The chain
  walk already resolves the animated base image and classifies each surface's
  lightmap state; non-combined surfaces now reuse those values instead of
  repeating texture animation and scanning light styles inside the ordinary
  brush helper. Rendering branches, dynamic-lightmap updates, texture bindings,
  and brush counters are unchanged. All 137 comparable build objects except
  `gl1_surf.o` are byte-identical to v1.54, including `rsx_gl.o` and every
  console/menu frontend object. Physical text and sustained-frame validation
  remain required; this release is a two-file update package.
- v1.54 replaces the physical console's remaining direct XDR vertex fetch for
  console, menu, HUD, and other orthographic geometry. The PPU still constructs
  complete batched strings in the 16 MiB rotating main-memory arena, but each
  completed batch is now fully evicted from the PPU cache and copied by an
  ordered RSX transfer into a separate eight-MiB local staging arena. The draw
  invalidates RSX's prior vertex-cache generation and binds only that immutable
  local copy. This removes both non-coherent direct fetch and same-address CPU
  rewrite from the stale/doubled text path. Allocation failure retains the
  v1.53 direct-XDR fallback, and `ps3_rsx_ui_local_stage 0` selects it for
  diagnosis. The local arena is recycled only after the preceding flip fence;
  ordinary frames add no GPU finish. All 137 comparable build objects except
  `rsx_gl.o` are byte-identical to v1.53, preserving its combined-lighting and
  world-chain optimizations. Physical-console text and pacing validation remain
  required, and the release is again a two-file update package.
- v1.53 removes redundant PPU work from the native combined-lighting world
  path while retaining v1.52's RSX sampler optimization. Each opaque world
  base-texture chain now resolves its base binding, overbright scale, and
  feature gate once instead of once per compatible surface. Its reusable
  lightmap bucket table clears only consumed heads and scans only the occupied
  page range rather than all 128 lightmap slots for every base image. Material
  grouping, draw order, lighting math, and fallback eligibility are unchanged.
  All 137 comparable build objects except `gl1_surf.o` are byte-identical to
  v1.52, including `rsx_gl.o` and the complete console/menu frontend. The
  release remains a two-file update package for the v1.50 full installation;
  duplicate/stale text and sustained frame pacing still require physical-console
  validation.
- v1.52 reduces native combined-lighting state traffic without touching UI
  geometry or visual ordering. Texture-sorted world chains hold the base image
  constant while iterating lightmap pages, but the v1.50 path treated both RSX
  samplers as one dirty unit and re-emitted the unchanged base descriptor at
  every page transition. Independent dirty tracking now reloads only the
  sampler whose texture or descriptor changed. Texture-cache invalidation
  remains explicit, copy-on-update hazard accounting is preserved for both
  units, and program transitions conservatively re-establish the pair.
  `lm_base_sets` and `lm_page_sets` in the 120-frame RSX trace expose the
  reduction on hardware. All 137 comparable build objects except `rsx_gl.o`
  are byte-identical to v1.51, including the complete console/menu frontend.
- v1.51 phase-locks input and simulation to the completed native VSync flip
  before sampling the next frame delta. This removes the independent software
  throttle followed by a second renderer flip wait that could drift into a
  missed refresh and produce slight movement jerk. `ps3_vsync_prewait 1` is
  the archived default; value 0 restores the bounded 50-microsecond polling
  fallback. RSX telemetry now reports average/maximum presentation cadence and
  counts intervals over 20 and 34 ms. The v1.50 full-surface clear, exact
  40-byte UI layout, rotating 16 MiB cache-published text stream, combined
  baked-lighting path, and all game data remain unchanged. The release is a
  two-file update package (PARAM.SFO and EBOOT.BIN) for an existing v1.50 full
  install; duplicate/stale text and pacing require physical-console validation.
- v1.50 restores the missing baked world lighting and targets the physical
  console's resolution-dependent frame drops with one native RSX draw per
  compatible opaque BSP surface. The established renderer drew each surface
  once for its base texture and again for its lightmap; physical-style depth
  replay rejected the equal-depth second pass, leaving the world effectively
  fullbright. A dedicated two-sampler Cg program now multiplies both textures
  in the first pass. In the repeatable 720p `base1` workload this removed about
  20 percent of frontend draws, packed vertices, and indices, at the cost of
  more material-pair submissions; the expected physical fill-rate benefit
  still requires console validation. `ps3_rsx_combined_lightmaps 0` restores
  the old path live and is archived as a hardware fallback. The implementation
  deliberately reuses the proven v1.47 40-byte vertex layout, so the rotating
  cache-coherent console/menu/HUD stream is byte-for-byte unchanged. Exact
  signed-EBOOT tests passed extended console text, Video Options, mono gameplay,
  top/bottom stereo, projected shadows, sound/input startup, screenshots, the
  disabled combined-path fallback, and full teardown with zero normal-frame
  GPU finishes. Native HDMI frame packing and physical text/frame-pacing remain
  hardware-validation items.
- v1.47 hardens v1.46's cache-coherent text path against physical RSX fetch
  retention. The mapped UI vertex stream grows from an 8 MiB frame-reset arena
  to a 16 MiB rotating arena: console/menu/HUD batches append across completed
  frames and reuse offset zero only at a flip-fenced frame boundary while still
  reserving the previous 8 MiB worst-case frame budget. This removes routine
  same-address-per-vblank rewrites without changing glyph geometry, draw order,
  stereo layout, or batching. A failed 16 MiB allocation/map automatically
  retries the proven 8 MiB coherent path; `ps3_rsx_ui_vertex_ring 0` plus
  `vid_restart` selects that fallback explicitly for diagnosis. Physical-console
  validation remains required.
- v1.46 is the boot-safe release repack of v1.45's complete-string UI batching,
  cache-coherent XDR text stream, and typed fan optimization. Two later timing
  and stereo-readback experiments were rejected: the desktop 1.2x VSync target
  produced an incomplete mono capture, while selecting a different stereo eye
  did not make tiled-surface diagnostics deterministic across repeated runs.
  The v1.46 linked program image therefore remains byte-for-byte equivalent to
  the tested v1.45 image (the newly generated NPDRM wrapper has its own hash);
  only install metadata advances so it can replace the crashing package.
  Physical-console text and pacing validation remain required.
- v1.45 extends v1.44's complete-string RSX path to right-aligned qmenu
  labels, center-print messages, and credits. In particular, Video Options no
  longer dispatches its green left-column labels one glyph at a time. The PS3
  main loop now sleeps for 50 rather than 5 microseconds between no-frame
  iterations, removing thousands of unnecessary LV2 sleep, command-buffer,
  and sysutil polls per second while keeping 50-microsecond scheduling
  resolution. An attempted residual-cadence scheduler was rejected during A/B
  testing because it produced horizontally incomplete world captures; the
  established render/network delta reset remains unchanged. An extended RPCS3
  Video Options run retained the exact v1.44
  layout with complete green/white text, stable repeated 120-frame telemetry,
  no normal-frame GPU finishes, and clean shutdown. The final exact signed
  v1.45 EBOOT then completed five telemetry intervals (600 rendered frames),
  loaded and rendered `base1`, submitted the projected shadow, produced a
  complete screenshot, retained zero normal-frame GPU finishes, and completed
  full sound/input/video teardown. Physical frame-pacing and text validation
  remain required.
- v1.44 submits each ordinary console row, notification, HUD line, inventory
  label, and left-to-right menu string as one contiguous native RSX glyph run.
  Spaces retain their advance without consuming geometry, atlas cells keep the
  half-texel inset, and the effective vertex color is resolved once per run.
  It removes repeated texture binds, function dispatch, arena reservations,
  and color conversion from every visible character. UI vertices now use a
  separate cache-coherent mapped-XDR arena by default, with explicit PPU cache
  publication before RSX consumption. This removes the physical stale-text
  path from repeatedly rewritten RSX-local memory without moving world/model
  geometry off its established stream; `ps3_rsx_ui_main_memory 0` plus
  `vid_restart` restores the old shared stream for diagnosis. New `ui_strings`
  and `ui_glyphs` telemetry counters expose whether the batched frontend path
  is active on hardware. The typed
  BSP/lightmap/water/sky fan path also emits its known sequential fan indices
  directly instead of re-entering generic primitive decoding for every index.
  The release is built through the corrected strip -> `sprxlinker` -> NPDRM
  pipeline retained from v1.43; physical-console text and pacing validation is
  still required.
- v1.43 replaces the withdrawn v1.41/v1.42 packages. Those EBOOTs were signed
  directly from the raw linker output and skipped PSL1GHT's required
  `sprxlinker` pass, leaving every import descriptor with a zero function count.
  GameOS therefore recognized `sysPrxForUser` but never bound its functions;
  newlib's pre-main allocator constructor recursed through the unresolved
  spinlock stub and returned to XMB. The standard strip -> sprxlinker -> NPDRM
  pipeline is restored and verified: the exact signed v1.43 EBOOT enters main,
  loads and renders `base1`, captures two 120-frame telemetry intervals, and
  completes the full sound/input/video shutdown before `sys_process_exit`.
  v1.41's direct non-retained RSX UI stream and top/bottom stereo mode remain.
  v1.42's direct native BSP/lightmap/water/sky fan path remains, with its common
  batching/fallback logic consolidated. Effective vertex color is now resolved
  once per direct UI or surface draw rather than once per vertex, reducing PPU
  work while preserving the existing shaders, lightmap blend, and draw order.
- v1.42 keeps v1.41's corrected stereoscopic output and direct native 2D path,
  then moves the highest-volume world submission path out of the generic GL
  client-array decoder. BSP base surfaces, lightmap chains, turbulent water,
  flowing surfaces, and sky faces now feed their known aligned position/UV
  layout directly into the existing native indexed or expanded RSX batches.
  Rendering order, geometry, shaders, texture state, and the diagnostic batch
  toggles are unchanged. The optimization removes repeated client-state,
  pointer, stride, and format validation work for roughly a thousand surface
  calls in a representative gameplay frame; physical-console frame pacing and
  visual correctness still require validation. Its distributed package was
  rejected because it omitted the final import-table link pass; v1.43
  supersedes it.
- v1.41 retains v1.40's corrected `VIDEO_STATE_ENABLED` boot handling and adds
  a standard 1280x720 top-and-bottom stereoscopic compatibility mode. Physical
  photographs and traces showed the remaining large "duplicated text" blocks
  were complete top/bottom eyes plus the 30-line HDMI packing gap, not duplicate
  glyph draws. Video Options can now select off, native frame packing, or
  top/bottom; the latter avoids 1280x1470 HDMI negotiation and is intended for
  a display, projector, capture device, or AVR that exposes the raw packed
  image. The RSX backend maps each full virtual eye into one 1280x360 half.
  Console characters, HUD elements, menu pictures, solid fills, fades, and
  cinematics now use a direct native RSX 2D-quad stream. This removes temporary
  GL client arrays and repeated state decoding from the UI path, eliminates the
  last retained-array variable from the stale-text investigation, and reduces
  PPU submission overhead. v1.40's single-write effective vertex-color packing
  remains active for world and model geometry.
- v1.40 fixes the v1.38 boot regression identified by physical logs. Blocking
  HDMI configuration successfully selected 0x81/1280x1470 and then reported
  video state 1 (`VIDEO_STATE_ENABLED`), but the inherited backend accepted only
  the transient/legacy value 0 and rejected renderer initialization with "No
  usable renderer found." Startup, restart, and render-size validation now
  accept both observed usable states while still rejecting busy/unknown output.
  The blocking stereo transition and its requested/confirmed mode telemetry are
  restored so the raw packed-eye text diagnosis can finally reach RSX, together
  with the single-pass effective vertex-color packing optimization.
- v1.39 is an emergency rollback to the physically proven v1.37 executable
  behavior. v1.38 returned directly to XMB before `main()` and did not update
  `boot-trace.log`, so its blocking video-mode confirmation and vertex-color
  packing experiment are both removed. Video configuration, vertex preparation,
  renderer synchronization, UI handling, lighting, sound, and performance paths
  are again identical to v1.37; only package/SFO version metadata is newer so
  the PS3 will install the rollback over v1.38.
- v1.38 identifies the remaining "duplicated text" from physical photographs
  and the matching trace as the complete 1280x1470 HDMI frame-packed image,
  not duplicate glyph geometry: the top and bottom 720-line eye images plus the
  30-line packing gap were being exposed as one ordinary picture. PS3 video
  configuration now blocks until GameOS completes the HDMI transition, confirms
  the requested mode id and dimensions before RSX allocates/presents matching
  buffers, and records the confirmed resolution, video state, and port type in
  `boot-trace.log`. An unconfirmed or inherited mode was explicitly reasserted.
  Vertex packing also folded GL_REPLACE and lightmap RGB scaling into the initial
  color write. This build was rejected after its physical PS3 EBOOT exited before
  `main()`; v1.39 fully rolls it back.
- v1.37 supersedes the incomplete v1.36 UI-cache diagnosis after physical PS3
  testing still showed doubled and retained console/menu text. Orthographic UI
  fans and quads now bypass the dynamic index arena entirely and expand into one
  deterministic triangle stream. Each 2D batch republishes and invalidates its
  vertex fetches, and charset UVs sample half a texel inside their 8x8 atlas
  cells. World/model geometry keeps indexed batching and its bandwidth savings.
- Audit the reported brightness difference without altering Quake II's valid
  lighting equation. OpenGL and RSX blend constants match exactly for the
  destination-times-lightmap pass, and native upload gamma already follows
  SDL's reciprocal exponent. Startup telemetry now records gamma, texture
  intensity, lightmap modulation, fullbright, and overbright values so the next
  physical trace can identify an archived-setting difference precisely.
- Remove recurring PPU heap traffic from the renderer hot path. Particle batches
  reuse workspaces bounded by `MAX_PARTICLES`; MD2 models and projected shadows
  share workspaces bounded by the loader's `MAX_VERTS`; flowing surfaces,
  offset lightmaps, and water warps use their existing 64-vertex bounds. This
  eliminates large per-frame particle allocation plus per-entity and per-surface
  malloc/free churn. Flip polling is tightened from 200 to 50 microseconds to
  reduce post-vblank command-generation latency without changing 60 Hz gameplay
  timing or the five-second recovery bound.
- v1.36 attempted to fix the physical-RSX stale/doubled UI geometry through
  dynamic vertex-cache synchronization, but physical testing proved that was
  insufficient and motivated v1.37's separate non-indexed 2D path.
  Dynamic glyph, menu, particle, model, and projected-shadow vertices reuse the
  same local-video-memory addresses each frame, but the old submission path
  invalidated RSX's vertex cache only for the optional XDR stream. RPCS3 did not
  reproduce that cache retention. Every dynamic local or XDR draw now publishes
  its completed writes, and the first consumer of each recycled arena generation
  invalidates RSX's prior vertex-cache view. This supersedes the v1.33-v1.35
  clear and scale hypotheses while retaining the full acquisition clear as a
  valid back-buffer lifecycle guard.
- Make projected shadows a completely explicit native pass: untextured,
  alpha-test and cull disabled, standard source-alpha blending, and depth writes
  disabled. Quake's existing 0.1-unit lift is retained without RSX polygon
  offset, which a controlled nearby-caster A/B showed could create long black
  streaks. The diagnostic
  trace is emitted only after real shadow vertices are submitted; the exact
  signed v1.36 EBOOT submitted a 602-vertex projected shadow in `base1`.
- Match engine and presentation cadence on PS3. Frame timing now comes from
  LV2's monotonic system timer, and VSync scheduling targets the actual 60 Hz
  output instead of the desktop renderer's 1.2x safety rate (72 Hz on a 60 Hz
  display). The native flip remains the final refresh-accurate throttle.
- Begin functional native PS3 sound. Fix the startup mixer lock that permanently
  starved the audio thread, sample the hardware cursor after each notification,
  zero new hardware blocks, make notification timeouts recoverable, maintain a
  16-bit stereo software ring, and stage all port/queue/thread cleanup. `soundinfo`
  now reports hardware blocks copied, blocks containing mixed samples, and zero
  blocks used while the mixer owns the ring. An exact-EBOOT RPCS3 run opened the
  48 kHz stereo port, advanced playback, and joined the audio thread cleanly;
  audible output and music still require physical-console validation.
- Fix stale/doubled console rows and retained translucent scene objects at the
  render-target lifecycle boundary. v1.33-v1.34 asked GL1 to clear color after
  each eye was selected, but physical RSX could execute that clear with raster
  state inherited from the preceding pass. v1.35 clears the complete newly
  bound back buffer, depth buffer, and stencil plane immediately after frame
  acquisition under an explicit full-target scissor and full write masks.
  Normal rendering no longer depends on console scale or previous-eye state.
- Restore Quake II's projected entity shadows. Hardware face culling, enabled
  by default after the early native builds, could reject the flattened shadow
  mesh even though the original model was visible. The shadow pass now disables
  culling locally and restores it immediately afterward. New and existing PS3
  configurations migrate once to `r_shadows 1`, and Video Options exposes a
  live **entity shadows** toggle; stencil overlap suppression remains optional.
- Split the experimental framebuffer selector into safe linear, tiled without
  Z-cull, and tiled plus Z-cull modes. Physical v1.34 validated the combined
  path only in frame-packed 3D, so mono can now isolate tiled color/depth layout
  from Z-cull metadata. All experimental modes remain session-only and a full
  relaunch restores linear targets.
- Remove redundant native clear work. The acquisition clear covers both packed
  eyes and their independent depth regions in one ordered GPU operation, so
  ordinary GL1 color/depth clears are skipped unless `r_clear`, z-trick, or a
  special stencil mode explicitly requires them. Renderer hot-path cvar caches,
  indexed batching, state filtering, and dynamic-texture reuse remain enabled.
- Retire v1.34's ineffective menu-scale workaround. Physical testing proved the
  reported doubled lines survived at 1x because they were retained framebuffer
  contents. v1.35 restores automatic menu scaling only for configurations that
  received the known v1.34 migration; preexisting and explicit choices remain
  untouched.
- Add a real **PS3 output** selector to Video Options. **720p performance**
  reconfigures HDMI scanout and the native RSX color/depth targets to 1280x720,
  reducing the framebuffer pixel workload by 55.6 percent compared with
  1920x1080. **system / XMB mode** restores the display mode that was active
  when the game started. Apply performs the required renderer restart; stereo
  continues to select its mandatory 720p frame-packed output.
- Expose the existing native tiled-color/depth plus Z-cull path as an
  experimental Video Options toggle. It remains session-only and defaults to
  linear targets after every relaunch, providing automatic recovery if a
  physical RSX rejects the layout. Cache native-renderer cvar objects as well,
  removing repeated name-table searches from each frame and dynamic-lightmap
  update while preserving all live diagnostic switches.
- Preserve the native renderer's complete Quake II lighting path and document
  what it renders: compiled RSX Cg vertex/fragment programs combine the GL1
  frontend's base textures, baked lightmaps, vertex modulation, and additive
  dynamic-light passes. This is the original Quake II lighting model rather
  than a modern material-shader replacement; no lightmap or shader stage was
  removed from the native build.
- Set a PS3 console-only 1x text scale instead of Yamagi's automatic integer
  scale. The inherited default enlarged every font texel 2x at 720p and 3x at
  1080p, making dense console lines resemble duplicated or overprinted text.
  Existing configurations using the automatic value migrate once; explicit
  user scale choices, menu scaling, and HUD scaling remain untouched.
- v1.33 first added a native color clear after controlled captures retained
  horizontal scanlines. Indexed/expanded/unbatched/filtering comparisons ruled
  out geometry batching, but the later physical v1.34 pass proved the remaining
  console artifact was retained target content rather than font scaling. v1.35
  therefore moves the clear to full-surface acquisition as described above.
- Add a direct **remap DualShock 3** entry to Options. Its binding screen uses
  Cross/Circle/Square/Triangle, Select, Start, L1/R1/L2/R2/L3/R3, and D-pad
  names; Cross selects an action, the next pressed controller button assigns
  it, Triangle clears it, and Circle exits. Select is now a regular bindable
  `BTN_BACK` key while retaining its menu behavior through a one-time migration
  for existing configurations. Bindings are archived in the normal config.
- Enable bounded 16-bit indexed RSX batching by default now that the native
  renderer has passed physical-console gameplay. Matched 120-frame RPCS3
  `base1` runs previously reduced streamed source vertices from 669,342 to
  406,272 (39.2 percent) while preserving the draw workload. Existing archived
  safety defaults are migrated once; `ps3_rsx_indexed_batch 0` remains the live
  expanded-geometry recovery path.
- Fix first-use geometry corruption in the optional mapped-main-memory RSX
  stream. v1.31 ordered PPU stores and invalidated RSX's vertex cache, but did
  not write dirty 128-byte PPU cache lines back to XDR before the GPU fetched
  them; RPCS3 reproduced this as a mostly black first `base1` capture followed
  by complete later maps after incidental cache eviction. v1.32 publishes each
  completed vertex/index range with `dcbst`, combines indexed ranges behind one
  memory barrier and one RSX vertex-cache invalidation, and retains the stronger
  `dcbf` path only for GPU-to-PPU screenshot DMA. The exact signed v1.32 EBOOT
  produced complete `base1`, `city1`, `waste1`, `space`, and `boss2` captures
  with mapped streams on both linear targets and tiled targets plus Z-cull.
  Both five-map sweeps shut down normally with zero normal-frame GPU-wide
  finishes or renderer faults. A further exact packaged-EBOOT run combined
  mapped streams, tiled targets/Z-cull, and 1280x1470 frame-packed stereo; its
  separately read top and bottom eyes were complete and distinct, with normal
  teardown and no normal-frame finish. Main-memory streaming remains opt-in
  pending a physical-console performance pass.
- Add opt-in native-renderer frame-time telemetry for physical-console A/B
  testing. `ps3_rsx_stats 1` now measures the completed-flip wait and PPU-side
  RSX command-generation interval with microsecond timestamps, then reports
  average/max milliseconds plus counts above 20 and 34 ms every 120 frames.
  Timing calls and trace writes remain absent when statistics are disabled.
  The exact signed v1.31 EBOOT completed a 240-frame RPCS3 `base1` smoke test,
  produced a complete framebuffer capture, emitted both timing records, and
  retained zero normal-frame GPU-wide finishes. A broader packaged-EBOOT sweep
  also produced complete `base1`, `city1`, `waste1`, `space`, and `boss2`
  framebuffers across repeated renderer/map registration, animated warp/sky
  materials, dynamic lightmap updates, entities, weapons, and UI. The same
  ordered sweep then passed with tiled color/depth targets and Z-cull active.
  Matched linear/tiled captures differed only in time-dependent animation and
  weapon-bob regions; both retained complete world geometry. Every measured
  interval retained zero normal-frame GPU-wide finishes, with no flip timeout
  or renderer fault.
- Make direct XMB Exit Game from active gameplay follow the physically proven
  menu-first lifecycle more closely. Once the sysutil callback commits to
  process termination, v1.31 skips local-server/game-module cleanup as well as
  the already guarded RSX teardown; LV2 reclaims those process-owned resources.
  Normal console `quit` still performs complete server, client, renderer, and
  common shutdown. Direct gameplay exit requires a physical-console retest;
  returning to the main menu first remains the known-safe fallback.
- Make native framebuffer readback reliable for both linear and tiled RSX
  targets. v1.30 keeps the allocation's tile base in the transfer source,
  expresses packed-eye selection through the blitter's source Y coordinate,
  and drains the 3D backend before the independent 2D engine reads the active
  surface. This removes the horizontal black/stale scanlines that command
  ordering alone could leave in captures. RPCS3 produced clean linear, tiled
  plus Z-cull, and tiled frame-packed top/bottom images. Screenshot readback
  remains an explicit diagnostic synchronization and adds no normal-frame
  finish. Tiled targets remain opt-in until a physical-console pass.
- Add coherent RSX framebuffer capture through a 1 MiB-aligned XDR staging
  surface. The renderer now orders a local-to-main surface blit after queued
  draws, invalidates the PPU cache before DMA, and reads the completed staging
  copy. This exposed that the older direct PPU reads of RSX-local scanout memory
  were black and therefore could not serve as image-fidelity comparisons.
- Reconcile Quake II's polygon winding with native RSX hardware face culling.
  An exhaustive framebuffer matrix identified direct GL FRONT/BACK mapping
  with `GCM_FRONTFACE_CCW` as the correct convention; the opposite equivalence
  class rejected almost the entire world. v1.29 enables the verified mapping by
  default. RPCS3 gameplay captures passed normal and left-handed weapon cull
  switches, camera movement, and both frame-packed stereo eyes with complete
  scenes and no normal-frame GPU-wide finishes. Session-only
  `ps3_rsx_hw_cull_mode 0` retains the v1.28 suppression path for recovery;
  modes 2 through 4 remain diagnostic mappings.
- Finish the frame-packed stereo correction: widen GL1's CPU side planes by the
  exact asymmetric projection offset, clear normally unused eye pixels to black,
  and copy only the selected 720-line eye during screenshots. RPCS3 advertised
  the native 1280x1470 mode and rendered two complete, distinct eyes with no
  stale menu/weapon imagery or magenta edge gaps. Stereo still forces VSync so
  each top/bottom pair is presented atomically.
- Keep `ps3_rsx_tiled_targets` disabled by default. RPCS3 reaches `base1` with
  tiled color/depth targets and Z-cull active, but the current screenshot blit
  does not detile that optional surface correctly; use the linear default for
  reliable capture and physical validation.
- Reuse native dynamic-texture allocations before their first sample in the
  current frame. `ps3_rsx_texture_frame_reuse 1` tracks the exact RSX pointer
  and frame serial, updates idle lightmap storage in place after the preceding
  flip fence, and retains copy-on-update renaming as soon as that allocation
  has entered a draw. Set it to `0` to restore v1.26's always-rename diagnostic
  path. Matched 120-frame RPCS3 `base1` runs produced the same draw workload
  and zero forced GPU finishes; reuse-on handled 87 of 131 updates
  in place and copied 2,816 KiB through 44 renames, versus 8,576 KiB through
  134 renames with reuse disabled.
- Keep native renderer teardown out of the XMB exit path itself. The renderer
  previously ran its blocking `rsxFinish()` and freed active allocations before
  `GLimp_Shutdown()` reached the existing process-exit guard, explaining why a
  menu-first exit could succeed while direct exit from gameplay froze. v1.26
  now relinquishes those process-owned renderer resources to LV2 as soon as the
  sysutil callback requests exit; normal `quit` and `vid_restart` still perform
  complete ordered teardown. Physical validation of direct gameplay exit is
  still required.
- Add an optional mapped-main-memory vertex/index stream. Set
  `ps3_rsx_stream_main_memory 1` and run `vid_restart` to place the 8 MiB vertex
  and 2 MiB index rings in one 1 MiB-aligned XDR mapping, avoiding PPU writes to
  RSX-local memory. Submission orders PPU stores, invalidates the RSX vertex
  cache, and binds both streams from `GCM_LOCATION_CELL`; mapping failure falls
  back to local memory. The switch is session-only and defaults off for its
  first hardware pass. Matched 120-frame indexed `base1` RPCS3 runs completed
  in both locations; normal shutdown also released the 10 MiB I/O mapping.
- Specialize native RSX client-array packing for the float layouts emitted by
  the GL1 frontend. This bypasses the generic component type and normalization
  decoder for the common path while retaining it for unusual or unaligned
  arrays; `ps3_rsx_fast_arrays 0` selects that fallback at the next frame.
  RPCS3 exercised the specialized path for every streamed `base1` vertex over
  120 frames. The final indexed combination packed 406,272 source
  vertices and emitted 668,514 indices across 3,298 RSX submissions. The
  generated PPU code uses alignment-safe single-float loads for source array
  components.
- Add an optional native tiled-render-target path with RSX tiled color buffers,
  tiled/compressed Z24S8 storage, and hardware Z-cull metadata. Set
  `ps3_rsx_tiled_targets 1` and run `vid_restart` to enable it; the linear path
  remains the default until physical-console validation, and is retained when
  legacy `gl1_ztrick` requests alternating depth directions. The test switch
  is session-only so a full relaunch always recovers linear targets. Warm-cache
  RPCS3 Vulkan A/B runs both rendered `base1` for 120 frames with identical 91,810
  GL1 draws, 3,331 hardware submissions, 669,342 streamed vertices, and 1,455
  filtered state calls; the tiled trace reported Z-cull active with no new
  RSX/Vulkan fault.
- Fix native frame-packed stereo persistence: acquire the frame on the first
  eye regardless of separation sign, route top/bottom regions by render-pass
  order so zero depth remains valid, and color/depth-clear each eye before
  drawing. Pump the system callback again immediately before presentation so
  a gameplay Exit Game request cannot queue one more display flip.
- Batch native triangle fans, strips, and quads with bounded 16-bit RSX index
  buffers. The `base1` RPCS3 Vulkan comparison streamed 406,734 vertices in
  indexed mode versus 669,342 through the expanded fallback for the same
  91,810 GL1 draws and 3,331 hardware submissions, a 39.2 percent reduction.
  It remains disabled by default for its first physical-console pass; set
  `ps3_rsx_indexed_batch 1` to enable it at the next frame boundary.
- Treat an empty Ogg music list as empty instead of reporting every map's CD
  track as out of range, while retaining track 02 as a valid one-file list.
- Handle the PS3 XMB Exit Game request through a registered sysutil callback.
  The main loop and bounded flip wait now pump system callbacks, then shut down
  configuration, audio, and input in order. The system-exit path deliberately
  avoids blocking RSX finish/free calls after XMB has reclaimed display flips;
  normal console `quit` and renderer restarts retain the complete teardown.
  Returning to the Quake II main menu before XMB Quit Game is the physically
  confirmed safe workaround; v1.23 also checks the callback before each flip
  to address direct exit from active gameplay.
  Keep lifecycle markers in the persistent trace while compiling out obsolete
  bring-up trace noise.
- Filter redundant native fixed-function state changes before they reach RSX.
  Repeated enable/disable, alpha, blend, depth, cull, stencil, raster, viewport,
  scissor, matrix-load, texture-environment, and sampler setters no longer emit
  duplicate methods or split a compatible triangle batch. Add opt-in
  `ps3_rsx_stats 1` telemetry every 120 frames, reporting GL1 API draws, actual
  RSX draws, batched calls, flushes, streamed vertices, and filtered setters.
  Set `ps3_rsx_state_filter 0` for the repeated-state diagnostic path.
- Batch compatible native RSX triangle draws across GL1 world and lightmap
  surface chains. Triangle fans, strips, quads, and lists are converted to one
  contiguous triangle stream, while matrix, texture, blend, depth, stencil,
  raster, clear, stereo-eye, readback, upload, and frame transitions flush the
  batch before changing state. This removes many small RSX submissions without
  reordering effects or weakening texture-update fences. Set
  `ps3_rsx_batch 0` to restore the one-draw-per-call diagnostic path.
- Make the native RSX Brightness control functional without adding fragment
  shader work: rebuild a PS3-only gamma table during texture upload and request
  one full renderer/texture reload when `vid_gamma` changes. Identity gamma
  remains byte-identical, invalid console values are clamped, and unchanged
  gamma no longer causes a redundant startup restart.
- Cache the native RSX shader pair, unchanged MVP constants, and sampler state
  across GL1 polygon draws. Texture uploads still invalidate RSX before their
  next sample, and copy-on-update renames still rebind the new local-memory
  offset, but repeated world polygons no longer emit redundant program,
  constant, texture-cache, and sampler methods.
- Expose native RSX non-power-of-two textures and optional anisotropic filtering
  to the GL1 frontend. World textures now exercise the RSX compatibility layer's
  CPU-generated mip chains without the legacy 256-pixel rescale/clamp path, and
  `r_anisotropic` maps 1x-16x requests onto the hardware sampler.
- Route same-size complete texture redefinitions through the flip-fenced update
  path, avoiding a full GPU finish and RSX reallocation for every cinematic or
  scrap-atlas refresh.
- Make native screenshot readback scale from the active stereo eye rather than
  sampling across the complete 1470-line frame-packed HDMI surface.
- Enable the native renderer's dynamic-lightmap texture renaming by default.
  Copy-on-update removes the per-upload `rsxFinish()` stall, retains superseded
  RSX allocations until the ordered display flip completes, and bounds retired
  texture memory to 16 MiB before falling back to one safe GPU synchronization.
  `ps3_rsx_texture_rename 0` restores fully synchronized uploads. RPCS3 reaches
  `base1` with native mip sampling and the renamed upload path active. A one-time
  settings revision migrates the archived v1.13 safety default to the new mode
  without overriding later user changes.
- Add native RSX mipmapped textures: retain the level-0 pitch across every
  linear mip level, track only contiguous uploaded levels, expose the complete
  chain to RSX, select all OpenGL mip-filter combinations, and open the sampler
  LOD range beyond level zero. CPU generation also supports the legacy
  `GL_GENERATE_MIPMAP` path.
- Enable native RSX HDMI 720p frame-packed stereo after the v1.13 2D baseline
  passed physical-console gameplay: route the two GL1 eye passes into the top
  and bottom 720-line regions of one 1280x1470 surface, protect the 30-line HDMI
  gap with per-eye scissoring, and present only after both eyes are complete.
- Fix the v1.12 native renderer's first-draw PPU alignment fault: use a
  naturally aligned 40-byte RSX vertex layout and explicit 32-bit stores while
  packing CPU client arrays into RSX-local memory.
- Decode client arrays by their declared byte, short, or float component type
  and normalize integer colors instead of assuming every element is a packed
  float vector.
- Add the v1.11 native RSX safety baseline after a physical-console freeze in
  v1.10: use a branchless fragment program, pause frame submission after a
  timed-out display flip, and record first-draw, flip, GPU-finish, and dynamic
  lightmap stages in the persistent boot trace.
- Keep RSX-local copy-on-update texture renaming available through
  `ps3_rsx_texture_rename`; the v1.13 physical baseline used `0` while v1.16
  advances the fenced path to the default performance mode.
- Preserve OpenGL texture-environment semantics in the native renderer: apply
  overbright RGB scaling only to COMBINE lightmap passes and patch fragment
  constants before binding Cg microcode, as required by the PSL1GHT command
  ordering used by its RSX samples.
- Add optional RSX-local texture renaming for dynamic lightmaps. Old allocations
  remain live until the ordered display flip proves earlier draws are complete,
  then are recycled at the next frame boundary.
- Reset the complete native compatibility state across renderer restarts and
  validate RSX address translation for render targets, shaders, textures, and
  streamed vertices before submitting their offsets to the GPU.
- Harden the second native RSX preview for physical-console testing: transpose
  OpenGL matrices for the Cg/RSX constant layout, restore scissor and depth-range
  state correctly, and implement the lightmap RGB scale used by the GL1 frontend.
- Move large GL texture, cinematic, and lightmap scratch arrays out of the small
  PPU thread stack, and stream draw vertices directly into the RSX arena.
- Align linear texture pitch for RSX requirements and synchronize dynamic
  texture updates conservatively so queued world draws cannot see a later
  lightmap block.
- Add the first native RSX renderer build: Quake II's established GL1 scene
  frontend now feeds direct RSX command buffers, Cg vertex/fragment programs,
  RSX-resident textures, a GPU vertex stream, and hardware depth/stencil,
  blending, culling, alpha testing, viewport, and scissor state.
- Keep native RSX behind the `PS3_NATIVE_RSX=1` build switch while hardware
  validation and remaining renderer fidelity work are in progress.
- Double-buffer the RSX scanout surfaces and follow the PSL1GHT flip contract:
  wait for the pending flip, write only to the non-displayed buffer, then queue
  that completed buffer. This prevents scanout from racing framebuffer writes.
- Make the Video menu VSync setting control 2D flip mode; frame-packed stereo
  forces VSync because both eyes must be presented as one synchronized pair.
- Move the savegame copy buffer out of the PPU stack, fixing a 64 KiB stack
  overflow after the first map finished spawning but before local connection.
- Make the PS3 New Game menu launch base1 directly instead of gating gameplay
  behind the legacy intro cinematic path.
- Move the CIN decoder's 128 KiB compressed-frame buffer and audio scratch
  buffer off the constrained PPU main-thread stack.
- Extend the persistent PS3 trace through server startup, BSP/entity loading,
  local connection, and client renderer registration.
- Add optional 1-to-5-worker SPU acceleration for palette conversion, output
  scaling, and stereoscopic frame packing, with bounded waits and automatic
  fallback to the existing PPU path.
- Replace the PS3 loopback-only network placeholder with nonblocking IPv4 UDP,
  DNS lookup, broadcast discovery, and client/server sockets.
- Add optional PlayStation Move gyro aiming and button input through PSL1GHT
  libgem, with runtime sensitivity, smoothing, and dead-zone controls.
- Add optional HDMI 720p stereoscopic frame-packing output and a runtime depth
  scaler to the PS3 software renderer.
- Scale software render modes to the physical PS3 display buffer, including
  1080p output, and document realistic performance profiles.
- Use the QUAKE2000 title/application ID and current PSL1GHT video-state API.

Quake II 8.01 to 8.10:
- The OpenGL 3.2 renderer now supports the classic underwater effect.
  Until now it was only available in the Software renderer.
- Add 'ref_gles3', an OpenGL ES 3.0 renderer lib. This is an variant
  of the OpenGL 3.2 renderer, using OpenGL ES instead of desktop OpenGL.
  It greatly enhances support for embedded GPUs like the RaspberryPI 4.
- The Software renderer now supports colored lightning. It can be
  enabled through `sw_colorlight`. (by 0lvin)
- Another round of timing fixes. Implement `cl_maxfps -1` (the new
  default, existing configs are not altered) to automatically select
  a known to be good packet framerate depending on the current renderer
  framerate. This solves some subtile problems with stuttering entities.
- Greatly improved support for joysticks and controllers. Devices can
  now be hotplugged at runtime, binding should be portable between
  differend controllers and there's a new menu for controller bindings.
  (by Jaime Moreira)
- Add the `r_2D_unfiltered` and `r_videos_unfiltered` cvars. When
  enabled 2D elements like the HUD and videos are not filtered, they
  appear pixeled.
- Add 'prefweap' command to select weapon by priority. (by Jaime
  Moreira)
- Support building with Microsoft Visual Studio 2015 and newer.
- Several game code fixes, mostly for stuck mechanics in fact2. (by
  BjossiAlfreds and maraakate)
- Fix two stuck monsters in hangar1 and jail5. (by Dremor8484 and
  BjossiAlfreds)
- Fix the `viewsize` cvar.

Quake II 8.00 to 8.01:
- Fix the game not starting when nonexistent dirs in the search path.
- Sync haptic joystick effects to the sound. (by 0lvin)
- Move several map bug fixes to entity files, add some more map bug
  fixes. Without entity files the maps are the same as in Vanilla Quake
  II. (by BjossiAlfreds)
- Play the correct demo loop after changing the current mod.
- Fix numbered paks with upper case letters added twice to the search
  path. Only Windows was affected.
- `g_footsteps 2` now only generate footstep sound when the player is on
  the ground entity and no longer when in water or air.
- Add a cvar `g_machinegun_norecoil' which disables the machinegun
  recoil effect. This is cheat protected. (by De-Deppe)
- Scale 8 bit textures when `r_scale8bittextures` is set. (by 0lvin)
- The game is no longer limited to 1000 FPS when running in timedemo
  mode. (by 0lvin)
- Optimise command line parser and fix several subtle bugs. (by
  BjossiAlfreds)
- Add `r_2D_unfiltered`, when set to `1` 2D elements aren't filtered.
- Fix soldiers never chowing their pain skin as long as they're alive.
- Fix relative paths in ZIP files not being found.
- Add `gamemode` command for changing between singleplayer, deathmach
  and coop. (by BjossiAlfreds)
- Show a download progress bar when `cl_http_show_dw_progress` is set to
  `1`. (by David Carlier)

Quake II 7.45 to 8.00:
- Client side support for the optional Vulkan renderer library.
- Non existent renderer libraries are now skipped over by the menu.
- Fix several bugs when loading autosaves.
- Bump the maximal number of OGG/Vorbis files to 128.
- Several fixes to the Barracuda Shark. (by BjossiAlfreds)
- 'vid_fullscreen' and 'r_mode' are no longer special, they require an
  explicit 'vid_restart' like every other cvar.
- Remove hardcoded map fixes and replace them by optional entity files.
  Add several newly discovered map fixes. (by BjossiAlfreds)
- Send the network protocol version to the server. This can be used by
  the server to support clients with different network protocol version.
  (by Knightmare)
- Force SDL to minimize the window when its focus is lost. This fixes
  several problem under Windows with SDL 2.0.14 and newer.
- Switch the semantics of the 'vid_fullscreen' cvar. '1' is now native
  fullscreen, like it was in Vanilla Quake II. '2' is desktop
  fullscreen. When desktop fullscreen is selected through the menu,
  'r_mode' is forced to '-2'.
- Add 'g_footsteps' to control the generation of footstep sound. This
  cvar is cheat protected. '1' is Vanilla Quake II behavior and the
  default. '0' never generates footstep sound, '2' always generates
  them.
- Support stereo wave files. (by 0lvin)
- Add 'cl_r1q2_lighstyle'. When set to '1' Yamagi Quake II uses the
  Vanilla Quake II light styles (for example yellow light for the
  Hyperblaster) instead of the default r1q2 light styles.
- Add a submenu to configure gamepad and joystick sensitivities. (by
  Larry Davis)
- Ensure that the config file is written before changing the active mod.
  This prevents config changes from getting lost.
- Overhaul the search path logic. Make sure that each directory is added
  only once.

Quake II 7.44 to 7.45:
- Fix a crash under windows when opening the games menu with mods
  installed.

Quake II 7.43 to 7.44:
- Fix some input option not getting saved.
- Limit busywaits to the full client. This lowers the cpu consumption
  of q2ded considerably.
- Rework the build system to be more distribution friendly. The base
  CFLAGS and LDFLAGS can now be overridden by the environment and by
  options passed to make. (by Simon McVittie)
- Fix some corner cases of broken IPv6 connectivity.
- Fix qport colliding between several Yamagi Quake II clients.
- Keyboard keys unknown to Yamagi Quake II can now be bound.
- Adaptive vsync is now supported by setting 'r_vsync' to '1'.
- Implement 'coop_pickup_weapons'. When set to '1', a weapon may be
  picked up by coop players if the player doesn't have the weapon in
  their inventory or no other player has already picked it up.
- In coop elevators wait for 'coop_elevator_delay' seconds.
- If 'cl_anglekick' is set '1' angle kicks are ignored. This breaks
  the gameplay a little bit, but helps against motion sickness. This
  cvar is cheat protected.
- Add 'listmaps' command and autocompletion for maps. (by JBerg)
- Make 'wait' in scripts wait for 17 ms. This fixes some movement
  makros.
- Support for Haiku. (by David Carlier)
- Add a 'mods' submenu. (by earth-metal)
- Add the 'vstr' command and 'nextdemo' cvar. Ported from ioquake3.
  (by Denis Pauk)

